// batch u: cartagena, plassey, quebec, panipat3, trenton, saratoga
Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "febre amarela": "A tropical disease spread by mosquitoes, with high fever, bleeding and yellowing skin. At the time, nobody knew it came from the mosquito, and it killed far more soldiers than bullets did.",
  "navio de linha": "A large warship with two or three decks of guns, built to fight in a line against equal ships. It was the main piece of the 18th-century fleets.",
  "fragata": "A warship lighter and faster than the ship of the line, used to scout, escort and patrol.",
  "granadeiros": "Soldiers picked from among the tallest and strongest of each regiment. They had once been the ones who threw grenades; by the 18th century they were the shock troops, and every British regiment had a company of them.",
  "asiento": "A contract by which the king of Spain gave another country the monopoly on bringing enslaved people to the Spanish colonies. In 1713, Great Britain got it.",
  "guerra da orelha de jenkins": "A war between Great Britain and Spain, from 1739 to 1748, that later merged into the War of the Austrian Succession. The name comes from Robert Jenkins, a British captain who said Spanish soldiers had cut off his ear in 1731.",
  "milícia": "A force made up of ordinary local people, called up when there is danger and who then go back to normal life. It trained less than professional soldiers.",
  "nababo": "The governor of a province of the Mughal Empire, in India, almost always from a hereditary family. In practice, the nawab of Bengal ruled like a king.",
  "sipaios": "Indian soldiers recruited, trained and paid by Europeans, with European-style weapons and uniforms. The British Company had thousands of them.",
  "companhia das índias": "The British East India Company: a private trading firm, licensed by the king, with its own forts, ships and army. It ended up ruling a large part of India.",
  "açude": "A large water tank dug into the ground, common in India, with earth banks around it. At Plassey, the two that lay between the armies served as shelter for the guns.",
  "pólvora": "A powder that explodes and makes cannons and muskets fire. When wet, it will not catch fire, which is why rain was an enemy to every army of the time.",
  "conselho de guerra": "A meeting in which the commander hears his officers before a grave decision. The decision is his, but the opinion of the group carries weight.",
  "diwani": "In the Mughal Empire, the right to collect the taxes of a province. Whoever held the diwani controlled the money. In 1765, the British Company received that of Bengal.",
  "guerra dos sete anos": "A war among the great European powers, from 1756 to 1763, fought in America and India as well. In North America, the British and the French fought over Canada and the Ohio River valley.",
  "infantaria ligeira": "Lighter, faster foot soldiers who fought spread out in front of the line or on its ends, shooting and avoiding hand-to-hand combat.",
  "highlanders": "Soldiers from the Scottish Highlands. In the 18th century, the British army formed whole regiments from them, in tartan kilts and with bagpipes.",
  "regulares": "The professional soldiers of a country's army, paid and trained all year round, as opposed to the militia.",
  "escaramuça": "A small, loose fight in which scattered groups trade shots before or beside the main battle.",
  "peshwa": "The hereditary prime minister of the Maratha Empire, who in fact governed in the king's name. He was based in Pune.",
  "maratas": "A people of western India (the region of Maharashtra) who, in the 18th century, formed a powerful confederation and took a large part of central and northern India from the Mughals.",
  "gardis": "Indian infantry trained in the European way, with muskets and drill discipline, in the Maratha army. The name probably comes from the French 'garde', because their officers learned from the French.",
  "durrani": "The empire founded in 1747 by Ahmad Shah Durrani, in Afghanistan. At Panipat, 'Durrani' and 'Afghan' mean the army he brought.",
  "rohilas": "Afghans settled in the Rohilkhand region of northern India, where they formed small kingdoms. They were enemies of the Marathas and allies of Ahmad Shah.",
  "zamburak": "A small, light swivel gun mounted on a camel's back. The camel knelt, the gunner fired, and the weapon was easy to move from one point to another.",
  "trincheira": "A deep ditch, with the earth thrown up in front, where soldiers shelter and fire.",
  "hessianos": "German soldiers from the region of Hesse, hired by the British government to fight in America. The Americans called all the German soldiers on the British side Hessians.",
  "bote durham": "A long, flat-bottomed boat with a pointed bow at both ends, which carried heavy cargo on the Delaware River. It was used to ferry Washington's army across.",
  "exército continental": "The army created by Congress in 1775 to fight the British, under the command of George Washington. The soldiers were enlisted for short terms, and many went home when their time was up.",
  "piquete": "A small outpost of sentries in front of the main body, to warn of any attack.",
  "fuzileiros": "Marksmen armed with rifles, with a rifled barrel that hit at far greater distances than a musket but was slower to reload. American rifles were famous for their accuracy.",
  "convenção": "A surrender agreement with conditions: the soldiers hand over their weapons, but receive promises in return, such as going home. Burgoyne insisted on calling a convention what was really a surrender.",
  "reconhecimento armado": "An advance by a large force to see where the enemy is and how strong he is, without wanting a full battle. Sometimes it becomes one."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  cartagena: {
    war: "War of Jenkins' Ear (1739–1748)",
    hook: "One of the largest armadas of its time against six ships, a wall, a mutilated admiral and yellow fever.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1741, Spain still dominated most of America, and the gold, silver and other riches of the colonies went to Europe through a few fortified ports. One of the most important was Cartagena de Indias, on the coast of present-day Colombia, the main port of the Viceroyalty of New Granada.",
        "Great Britain wanted a slice of that trade. Under the Treaty of Utrecht, of 1713, it received the [[asiento|asiento]], the exclusive right to sell enslaved people to the Spanish colonies, and permission to send one ship of merchandise as well, the navío de permiso. The British smuggled heavily, and the Spanish coast guards searched and seized their ships.",
        "In this climate, the story of Robert Jenkins became famous: a captain who said Spanish soldiers had cut off his ear in 1731. The case became a rallying cry for those who wanted war, and gave its name to the [[War of Jenkins' Ear|guerra da orelha de jenkins]]. The story helped win public support for the war.",
        { h: "The road to Cartagena" },
        { timeline: [
          ["23 Oct. 1739", "Great Britain declares war on Spain."],
          ["22 Nov. 1739", "Admiral Vernon takes Porto Bello, in Panama, with six ships."],
          ["Mar.–May 1740", "Vernon appears before Cartagena twice, the second time with 13 warships, to test the defenses."],
          ["13 Mar. 1741", "The great armada arrives off Cartagena."],
          ["5 Apr.", "The British take the fort of Boca Chica, at the entrance to the bay."],
          ["20 Apr.", "The night assault on the fort of San Lázaro fails."],
          ["25 Apr.", "The British council decides to withdraw."],
          ["Mid-May", "The fleet leaves."]
        ]},
        "The victory at Porto Bello made Vernon a popular hero in England, and the government put together an enormous expedition to strike at the center of Spanish power in the Caribbean. It sailed from England under Lord Cathcart, who died of dysentery on the way. Command of the land troops passed to General Thomas Wentworth, who had never commanded soldiers in combat.",
        "In Jamaica, the [[council of war|conselho de guerra]] accepted Vernon's plan to attack Cartagena rather than Havana. Havana had the shipyard the British navy lacked, and was favored by some ministers in London."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Blas de Lezo, 52 years old" },
        "Born in 1689 in Pasajes, in the Basque Country, he joined the navy at 12. A life of combat cost him dearly: a cannonball took off the lower part of his left leg at Vélez-Málaga (1704), he lost an eye at Toulon (1707), and he lost the use of his right arm at Barcelona (1714). He was like this before turning 25. He returned to Cartagena in 1737, as commander of the Spanish squadron of the port.",
        "Lezo believed the defense had to begin at the mouth of the bay. If that failed, his plan was to fall back fighting, from position to position, to buy time until the rains and disease worked against the attackers.",
        { h: "Sebastián de Eslava, the viceroy" },
        "Eslava commanded the defense and was Lezo's superior. The two disagreed: Lezo wanted to defend Boca Chica with everything, and Eslava left the forward posts with few men, which made the British landing easier. Lezo's fame has grown a great deal in recent years, but the man in overall command was Eslava.",
        { h: "Edward Vernon and Thomas Wentworth" },
        "Vernon was the admiral: popular, confident and a political enemy of Prime Minister Robert Walpole. Wentworth commanded the land troops without ever having led soldiers in a battle. The relationship between the two, and between the army and the navy, was bad throughout the campaign, with each side blaming the other. The historian John Fortescue considers Vernon chiefly responsible for this friction.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Spanish side, the engineer Carlos Desnaux defended the fort of San Luis, at Boca Chica. On the British side, Admiral Chaloner Ogle commanded one of the divisions of the fleet, and Colonels Wynyard and Grant led the grenadiers and the main body in the final assault."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British gathered between 27,400 and 30,000 men, depending on the source (the chronicler Beatson gives 27,398). There were between 124 and 186 vessels, depending on how you count, including 29 [[ships of the line|navio de linha]], [[frigates|fragata]] and dozens of troop transports. About 12,000 soldiers went ashore: the 15th and 24th infantry regiments, some 6,000 marines and about 3,600 American colonists.",
        "On the Spanish side, the most cited count ranges from 3,000 to 4,000 men: 3,150 fighters in one count, close to 4,000 if we add the ships' crews. They were regular soldiers, [[militiamen|milícia]], companies of Black and mulatto men and about 600 Indigenous archers, who also worked as auxiliaries. Lezo had six ships of the line: Galicia (70 guns), San Carlos (66), Conquistador (66), África (60), San Felipe (60) and Dragón (60).",
        { note: "The numbers for this campaign vary widely from source to source, on both sides. Here we use the ranges that historians cite, not a single number." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The bay of Cartagena is wide and sheltered. The only deep entrance was Boca Chica, a channel so narrow that it let one ship through at a time. Two forts, facing each other, guarded it: San Luis (four [[bastions|baluarte]], about 49 guns and some 300 soldiers) and San José (13 guns and 150 soldiers), with a floating barrier between them. Anyone who got through reached an inner bay and then the city, surrounded by walls with about 160 guns, plus 140 more in the suburbs.",
        "South of the city, on top of a hill, stood the fort of San Lázaro, today called San Felipe de Barajas. If the British took it, they could bombard the city at will and force it to surrender quickly. That is why Lezo saw it as the decisive point.",
        { h: "Boca Chica" },
        "The armada arrived on 13 March. Over the following days, Vernon silenced batteries and attacked smaller posts, such as Punta Abanicos, and Ogle's division drove the defenders out of two small forts, San Iago and San Felipe, at a cost of about 120 casualties. The grenadiers landed on the night of 21 March, and the rest of the troops on the 22nd.",
        "The fort of San Luis was pounded for days and lost part of its walls. One source speaks of three days of bombardment until the breach opened; another counts sixteen days of attack on the fort. Lezo blocked the channel: he sank two ships (the sources disagree on which) and set fire to the Galicia, which the British captured before sinking it.",
        "On 5 April, when the British attacked San Luis by land and sea, the Spanish had already fallen back to the inner bay. The fleet entered the bay. The cost had already been high: at Boca Chica, 120 killed and wounded in combat, 250 dead of fever and about 600 sick in the hospital.",
        { h: "Between the bay and the hill" },
        "On 16 April, Wentworth landed at Texar de Gracias, near the hill of San Lázaro. At this point time was on the Spanish side, as Lezo had planned. He ordered a [[trench|trincheira]] dug around the last bastion of San Lázaro and the field of fire in front cleared. He placed about 650 soldiers in the trench, 300 inside the fort and a [[reserve|reserva]] of 200 marines and sailors.",
        { h: "The night of 20 April" },
        "The British decided to attack San Lázaro at night, without first opening a breach, because they no longer had an engineer to set up a [[battery|bateria]]. The plan used several columns. In front, 50 picked men, and behind them 450 [[grenadiers|granadeiros]] under Colonel Wynyard, who would scale the lowest wall, on the south side. The main body, about 1,000 men from the 15th and 24th regiments, under Colonel Grant, would attack the north face. Behind came smaller groups, unarmed American colonists carrying ladders and wool sacks to fill the ditch, and a reserve of 500 marines.",
        "Everything went wrong. The march was late, and the attack only began near dawn, around 4 a.m. Two Spanish deserters acted as guides and led the grenadier column to the south side, over steep ground, where it took a point-blank [[volley|salva]]. On the north side, Grant fell early, and the soldiers, without a leader, only traded shots. Most of the colonists dropped their ladders and took cover, and those that arrived were some ten feet (about three meters) too short for the walls.",
        "The sun rose in the middle of the fight, and the guns of the city began to fire on the attackers. Around 8 a.m., a Spanish column came out of the city and threatened to cut off the retreat. Wentworth ordered a withdrawal. Of about 2,000 men, some 600 were dead or wounded.",
        { h: "The disease" },
        "Meanwhile, [[yellow fever|febre amarela]] and other tropical diseases were bringing down the British more than the gunfire. Of some 6,600 soldiers ashore, only 3,200 were fit to fight at the end of the campaign. On 25 April, the council decided to return to Jamaica. The fleet left in mid-May, and some ships were burned because there were not enough crews to sail them."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The British lost the battle and the expedition. Losses vary widely by source: from 9,500 to 11,500 dead in one count, up to 18,000 or 20,000 dead or incapacitated in others, most of them from disease. The historian Geggus calculates that between 70% and 77% of the roughly 12,000 men who landed died. Of the 3,600 American colonists, only about 300 came back.",
        "On the Spanish side, between 600 and 800 men died, and some 1,200 were wounded or sick, according to estimates. Lezo's six ships were lost. The city, however, remained standing, and New Granada stayed Spanish until the wars of independence, about 80 years later. Lezo died on 7 September 1741, four months after the battle. A letter from the time attributes his death to typhus, and the location of his tomb is unknown.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "News of the defeat reached Europe at the end of June 1741. Walpole's enemies accused him of not having supported Vernon. Parliament debated an inquiry in January 1742, and Walpole resigned in the first week of February, after more than 20 years in power. The defeat helped bring him down. Lord Hardwicke summed up what it showed: from then on, America would have to be fought for in Europe.",
        "The war gradually merged into the great European conflict, the War of the Austrian Succession. For those who study war, Cartagena shows three things: the value of defending in layers, in which each line forces the enemy to spend time; the weight of tropical diseases; and the price of an operation in which the army and the navy do not get along.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The medals: before knowing the result, English manufacturers, many of them button makers, made medals to commemorate Vernon's 'victory'. There are as many as eleven types, none official, and one of them shows Lezo kneeling and handing over his sword. They did exist, but they were made without the government's involvement and ahead of time." },
        { note: "The legend of the king: it is said that King George II forbade anyone to write about the defeat. The Spanish historian Francisco Hernando Muñoz Atuesta says this is absolutely false. Many popular books about Lezo are impressionistic and not very reliable; the nicknames 'Patapalo' and 'Mediohombre', for example, have no proof of having been used in his lifetime." },
        { note: "Mount Vernon: among the few colonists who came back was Lawrence Washington, George's half-brother. He gave his estate in Virginia the name of the admiral, and the house later became famous as Mount Vernon." },
        { note: "The novel: the writer Tobias Smollett was on the expedition, and the Cartagena episode appears in his novel The Adventures of Roderick Random." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  plassey: {
    war: "Conquest of Bengal by the British East India Company (1756–1757)",
    hook: "About 3,000 men against about 50,000, in a battle whose outcome had already been, in large part, agreed in advance.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the mid-18th century, Bengal, in eastern India, was one of the richest regions in the world. Officially, it was a province of the [[Mughal|mogol]] Empire, governed by a [[nawab|nababo]] who, in practice, ruled like a king. Europeans traded there through private companies. The [[British East India Company|companhia das índias]] had forts, ships and Indian soldiers, the [[sepoys|sipaios]]. The French company was based at Chandannagar.",
        "In 1756, the [[Seven Years' War|guerra dos sete anos]] was beginning, and the British strengthened the defenses of Calcutta for fear of the French. The nawab Siraj ud-Daulah, who had come to power that year, ordered them to stop. The British did not obey, and in June he took Calcutta. After the fall, British prisoners were locked in a small cell of the fort, the 'Black Hole of Calcutta'. How many died is hotly disputed: the best-known account, by a survivor, speaks of 123 dead out of 146 prisoners, and modern historians think the number was much smaller.",
        { h: "The road to Plassey" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Jun. 1756", "Siraj ud-Daulah takes Calcutta."],
          ["Jan. 1757", "Robert Clive and Admiral Watson retake the city."],
          ["Mar. 1757", "The British take the French post of Chandannagar."],
          ["21 Jun.", "Council of war at Katwa: most of the officers vote against attacking at once."],
          ["22 Jun.", "The army crosses the river."],
          ["23 Jun.", "The battle of Plassey."]
        ]},
        { h: "The conspiracy" },
        "Siraj distrusted the British, but he had other problems: Afghan and Maratha threats, and a court full of discontented people. It was in this court that Clive found allies. The commander Mir Jafar, the general Rai Durlabh, Yar Lutuf Khan, the Jagat Seth bankers and the merchant Omichund joined the conspiracy. Mir Jafar wanted the throne, and in exchange he would help the British.",
        "The British signed two treaties: a real one, and a false one, made to deceive Omichund, who expected a share of the treasure. Admiral Watson's signature was forged on the false document, which Clive himself admitted to Parliament in 1773."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Robert Clive, 31 years old" },
        "He arrived in India at 18 as a clerk of the Company and later moved into a military career. At Plassey, he had very few men, and he knew that everything depended on Mir Jafar keeping the deal. The day before, at the council of war, he himself voted against attacking. After about an hour of reflection, he changed his mind and ordered the army across the river.",
        { h: "Siraj ud-Daulah, under 30 years old" },
        "He was the grandson and successor of Alivardi Khan, the previous nawab. He came to the throne in 1756, young, in a government full of rivals. He had an enormous army, but could not trust those who commanded it: the three chiefs who led most of it, Mir Jafar, Yar Lutuf Khan and Rai Durlabh, were in the conspiracy.",
        { h: "Mir Jafar" },
        "Commander of the nawab's army and chief of its left wing, the one closest to the British. During the battle, he did not attack, and sent word to Clive to advance. He was later proclaimed nawab.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Bengal side, Mir Madan Khan, the loyal commander who died in the fighting, and Mohan Lal commanded 5,000 cavalry and 7,000 infantry, behind Saint-Frais's French gunners. Rai Durlabh and Yar Lutuf Khan, conspirators, commanded the center and the right. On the British side, Major Eyre Coote voted in favor of attacking and commanded the grenadiers who took the hill at the end of the battle, and Major Kilpatrick advanced without orders at the decisive moment."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The British had about 3,100 men: 750 Europeans, 100 topasses (soldiers of Indo-Portuguese origin), 2,100 sepoys, 100 gunners and 50 sailors, with 8 guns.",
        "The most cited figure for the nawab's army is about 50,000 men: 16,000 to 18,000 cavalry and some 35,000 infantry, with 40 to 53 guns and about ten [[war elephants|elefantes de guerra]]. One account speaks of as many as 300 pieces of artillery, a number that seems exaggerated. There were also about 50 French gunners, commanded by Saint-Frais, who operated the most advanced guns.",
        { note: "The sources do not agree on the size of the nawab's army, nor on the losses. British losses appear as 22 killed and 50 wounded in one account, 27 killed and 50 wounded in another." },
        { h: "The terrain and the march" },
        "Plassey (Palashi) lies on the bank of the Hooghly River, an arm of the Ganges, some 150 km north of Calcutta. Clive reached Katwa on 19 June, after Coote took the fort there. On the night of 22 to 23 June, the army crossed the river and arrived beyond Plassey at about 1 a.m.",
        "The British occupied the Laksha Bagh, a mango grove of about 730 by 270 meters, surrounded by a ditch and a mud wall, set diagonally to the river. Clive stayed in a hunting lodge, with a masonry wall, to the north. About 1.6 km away lay the nawab's entrenched camp: a [[trench|trincheira]] of 180 meters perpendicular to the river and then some 5 km to the northeast, with a gun [[redoubt|reduto]] at the bend. Between the two armies there were two [[tanks|açude]], and near the larger one stood the French.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at 8 a.m., the French fired the first shot, and the nawab's artillery opened fire. By 8:30, the British had already lost 10 Europeans and 20 sepoys, and Clive pulled the army back behind the wall of the grove.",
        "Second, after about three hours with no progress, Clive's officers decided to wait for night and attack the camp at midnight.",
        "Third, a heavy rain fell. The nawab's guns were uncovered, and the [[gunpowder|pólvora]] got wet. The British had covered theirs with tarpaulins, and their guns kept firing.",
        "Fourth, when the rain eased, Mir Madan launched the cavalry. British [[grapeshot|metralha]] mortally wounded him and the charge fell apart.",
        "Fifth, Siraj, frightened by the death of Mir Madan, called Mir Jafar, who sent word to Clive to advance. Rai Durlabh persuaded the nawab to fall back behind the trench and return to Murshidabad. Siraj left with about 2,000 horsemen. At about 2 p.m., the army stopped firing and began to retreat, leaving the French without support.",
        "Sixth, Major Kilpatrick, whom Clive had left in command, advanced on his own against Saint-Frais. Clive reprimanded him, but then brought up the whole army. At 3 p.m., the French position fell, and the gunners fell back to the redoubt.",
        "Seventh, Mir Jafar's division remained still. At one point, Clive saw a column move, thought it threatened his baggage and sent a detachment to watch it. Only later did he understand that it was Mir Jafar's division.",
        "Eighth, at 4:30 p.m., Coote's grenadiers took the small wooded hill, east of the redoubt, without meeting resistance. At 5 p.m., the British held the redoubt, the trench and the camp. At night they stopped some 10 km farther on, near Daudpur."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The British lost a few dozen men. Clive estimated the nawab's losses at about 500, including important officers. The next day, 24 June, Clive met Mir Jafar at Daudpur and hailed him as nawab. On 29 June, he arrived at Murshidabad and installed him on the throne.",
        "Siraj fled by boat, wanting to reach Patna, but was captured at Rajmahal on 2 July and killed on the orders of Mir Jafar's son, Miran. The new treaty gave the Company lands around Calcutta and an indemnity of 22 million rupees (about 2.75 million pounds), half of it paid at once. Siraj's treasure, however, was far smaller than expected. Omichund learned that he would receive nothing and, according to the accounts, went mad.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The French lost their importance in Bengal. In 1759, Clive also defeated a Dutch force at Chinsura, which Mir Jafar had encouraged to challenge the British. In the following years, the Company took control of Bengal, the richest region of India: in 1764, it won at Buxar, and in 1765 it received the [[diwani|diwani]] of the province, that is, the right to collect its taxes.",
        "Many historians see Plassey as the beginning of British rule in India. It is a convenient date, but it is not the day India changed hands: control of Bengal came in stages, and direct British government only began in 1858, after the great revolt of 1857.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Battle or conspiracy? Many historians point out that the result owes more to the deal with Mir Jafar and to the nawab's flight than to the fighting itself. The numbers help explain: the British lost a few dozen men, and the Bengal army lost about 500, out of some 50,000. Mir Jafar's division never actually fought." },
        { note: "The deception of Omichund: in 1773, Clive defended in Parliament the false treaty and the forged signature, saying it was a matter of good policy and justice." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  quebec: {
    war: "The Seven Years' War in North America (1756–1763)",
    hook: "In less than half an hour, on a field beside Quebec, French Canada lost its capital.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 18th century, North America was contested by two powers. France controlled Canada and the Saint Lawrence River valley, New France. Great Britain had the thirteen colonies of the east coast, with far more people than New France. The [[Seven Years' War|guerra dos sete anos]], which began in 1756, brought the confrontation to America, where it is also called the French and Indian War.",
        "Quebec stood on a cliff above the Saint Lawrence and was the capital of New France. Whoever controlled it controlled the mouth of the river and, with it, the road to the interior. In 1758, the British took the fortress of Louisbourg and Fort Frontenac, which opened the way by sea to Quebec.",
        { h: "The road to the plain" },
        { timeline: [
          ["1758", "The British take Louisbourg and Fort Frontenac."],
          ["Jun. 1759", "The British fleet arrives at Quebec and the siege begins."],
          ["31 Jul.", "The British attack at Montmorency fails."],
          ["12–13 Sep.", "At night, the British climb the cliff."],
          ["13 Sep.", "The battle, around 10 a.m."],
          ["18 Sep.", "Quebec surrenders."]
        ]},
        "James Wolfe had expected to receive 12,000 soldiers, but received about 7,000 regular soldiers, plus some 400 officers and 300 gunners. They came in a fleet under Admiral Charles Saunders, with 49 ships and another 140 smaller vessels. On 31 July, Wolfe attacked the French defenses at Montmorency and lost about 450 men, against some 60 French.",
        "After that, Wolfe ordered attacks on the settlements along the river. Sick and frustrated by the defensive stance of Montcalm, who refused to come out of the trenches, he understood that he had to attack by the end of September."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "James Wolfe, 32 years old" },
        "A career officer, he had already fought at Louisbourg in 1758. At Quebec, he was ill, and the failures of the summer were consuming him. It was his last chance before the fleet had to go back, and he changed the landing plan: the sources do not explain well why.",
        { h: "Louis-Joseph de Montcalm, 47 years old" },
        "Commander of the French regular troops in Canada, a veteran soldier who had won at Oswego, William Henry and Carillon. At Quebec, he preferred to wait behind the defenses. He quarreled with Governor Vaudreuil, the civil chief of New France, who blamed him for the defeat afterward.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the French side, Louis-Antoine de Bougainville stayed upriver, at Cap-Rouge, with about 3,000 men, and Vergor guarded the cliff path. On the British side, William Howe, the same man who years later would command the British army in the American Revolution, climbed the cliff with 24 volunteers. Brigadiers Monckton, Murray and Townshend commanded on the day."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Counts differ. An official Canadian source, citing the historian André Charbonneau, gives 4,426 men on the British side, all from the regular army, with two light guns. Another source gives 4,400. On the French side, one source speaks of 3,400 men (1,900 regulars and 1,500 [[militia|milícia]] and Indigenous warriors) and another of about 4,400, including regulars from the colonial troops. Montcalm had some 13,390 men spread between Quebec and Beauport, but most of them did not fight.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "Quebec stands on a cliff, on the north bank of the river. West of the city, on top of the cliff, lie the Plains of Abraham, an open field where the British formed their line. The name comes from a 17th-century river pilot, Abraham Martin, who owned land there.",
        { h: "The night of the landing" },
        "On 12 September, Wolfe chose the Anse-au-Foulon, a cove below a 53-meter cliff, some 3 km upriver from Cape Diamond. Bougainville was farther along, at Cap-Rouge. On the path from the Foulon there was a guard post with about 100 militiamen (perhaps only 40), under Vergor. Meanwhile, Saunders made a diversionary move in front of Montmorency, to hold Montcalm's attention.",
        "The boats went down the river in the dark. According to the accounts, when a French sentry challenged them, an officer of the [[Highlanders|highlanders]] answered in French. The currents carried the boats a little off course, and the soldiers landed at the foot of a slope. A group of 24 volunteers, under William Howe, climbed it, took Vergor's post and secured the heights. Wolfe climbed an hour later, by an easier path.",
        { h: "The battle in four moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at dawn, there were about 4,500 British on the heights. Wolfe formed a shallow line, about 1,000 meters long, two ranks deep, anchored on the edge of the cliff. From right to left: the 35th regiment, the Louisbourg grenadiers, the 28th (beside which Wolfe stood), the 43rd, the 47th, Fraser's [[Highlanders|highlanders]] and the 58th. The 15th was placed at an angle on the left, to guard the flank, and the 48th stood in [[reserve|reserva]]. A small gun was dragged up the cliff.",
        "Second, Montcalm learned of the landing in the morning, on seeing the red line before the city. Soldiers came out of Quebec, but the governor released only three guns from the walls. Montcalm formed his line, from right to left: a battalion of militia, the regiments of Béarn, La Sarre, Guyenne, Languedoc and Roussillon, and another battalion of militia, with Canadian militiamen and Indigenous allies covering the ends.",
        "Third, around 10 a.m., Montcalm attacked without waiting for Bougainville or the troops from Beauport. The regular regiments advanced three ranks deep, and the weakest in a column. Meanwhile, the guns fired, and the marksmen on the ends traded [[skirmish|escaramuça]] fire with the British [[light infantry|infantaria ligeira]]. The British, lying on the ground to avoid the fire, waited.",
        "Fourth, the French fired from far away, to no effect, and kept advancing. The British held their fire until the French were about 30 to 35 yards away (some 30 meters) and delivered two [[volleys|salva]]. After the second, the French line broke. The British advanced, and the French fled.",
        { h: "The two generals" },
        "Wolfe was wounded during the advance, in the chest and abdomen, and died shortly afterward, on the field. According to tradition, he learned of the French flight before he died. Montcalm was hit during the retreat, carried into the city and died the next morning. He was buried in a crater opened by a bomb, in the chapel of the Ursulines.",
        "The 78th, Fraser's highlanders, pursued the French, but came under heavy fire near the city. It is cited as the last successful highlander charge. Bougainville arrived with his men from the rear, but Townshend, who had taken command, turned two battalions against him, and Bougainville withdrew."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The battle lasted less than half an hour. British losses were about 60 killed and 600 wounded. French losses were 644 to 716 killed and wounded, including 13 officers, plus some 350 prisoners, according to one source; another speaks of 116 killed, 600 wounded and 350 prisoners.",
        "Vaudreuil ordered Quebec and Beauport evacuated and blamed Montcalm for the defeat. The commander of the city, Ramezay, surrendered Quebec, and on 18 September the terms of capitulation were signed.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The war went on. In April 1760, General Lévis defeated the British at Sainte-Foy, in a fight that cost about 850 casualties to the French and 1,100 to the British, and besieged Quebec. But the British fleet arrived in mid-May, and the British naval victory in Quiberon Bay, at the end of 1759, had prevented France from sending reinforcements. Montreal surrendered on 8 September 1760.",
        "Under the Treaty of Paris, of 1763, New France passed to Great Britain. In 1774, the Quebec Act guaranteed the inhabitants the Catholic religion and French civil law, which is why Quebec speaks French to this day. With France out of the continent, the British colonists lost a rival that had kept them dependent on the Crown, and some historians link this to the American Revolution, 16 years later.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The legends of Wolfe: it is said that he recited Gray's Elegy, which he liked very much, in the boat on the night of the landing. This and other stories about Wolfe are now treated as myth." },
        { note: "The canceled reenactment: in 2009, the 250th anniversary of the battle was scheduled to include a reenactment in Quebec, canceled over threats to public order. Only a reading of texts was held, without incident. In 2017, a cannonball from the siege, still loaded, was found in the city." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  panipat3: {
    war: "War between the Maratha Empire and the Durrani Empire (1757–1761)",
    hook: "A starving army came out of its trenches and, in a single day, lost the dream of ruling India.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Panipat, some 90 km north of Delhi, had already been the stage for two battles that changed India: that of 1526, in which Babur founded the Mughal Empire, and that of 1556, in which Akbar saved it. In 1761, the third took place.",
        "After 1707, the [[Mughal|mogol]] Empire was in decline, and those on the rise were the [[Marathas|maratas]], a confederation of western India headed by the [[peshwa|peshwa]], in Pune. Between 1757 and 1758, they advanced north, occupied Lahore, reached Attock, in the far northwest, and expelled Timur Shah, son of Ahmad Shah Durrani, the founder of the [[Durrani Empire|durrani]], in Afghanistan.",
        "Muslim leaders in Delhi, among them the cleric Shah Waliullah, asked Ahmad Shah, also known as Abdali, to rein in the Marathas. He put together a coalition: the [[Rohillas|rohilas]] of Najib ud-Daulah, the nawab Shuja-ud-Daula of Awadh, and Mughal nobles.",
        { h: "The road to Panipat" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Jan. 1760", "Dattaji Shinde, a Maratha chief, is killed near Delhi."],
          ["Late Jul. 1760", "Bhau arrives in Delhi with the Maratha army."],
          ["17 Oct. 1760", "The Marathas take the fort of Kunjpura and kill the Afghan garrison."],
          ["Late Oct. 1760", "Ahmad Shah crosses the Yamuna River and cuts the Marathas' supplies."],
          ["Nov. 1760 – Jan. 1761", "The two armies face each other at Panipat."],
          ["14 Jan. 1761", "The battle."]
        ]},
        "The peshwa Balaji Baji Rao sent his cousin, Sadashivrao Bhau, as commander in chief, after his brother Raghunathrao refused to go north. Historians tend to criticize Bhau's leadership. Suraj Mal, the king of the Jats, an ally of the Marathas, advised guerrilla warfare and the protection of the families and supplies. Bhau refused, thinking this dishonorable, and the Jats withdrew.",
        "At Kunjpura, in October 1760, the Marathas took the fort, killed the commanders and executed or enslaved the garrison within sight of Abdali's camp. Abdali crossed the Yamuna, in flood, between 23 and 25 October, near Baghpat, without being disturbed. His men cut the Marathas' supply lines, and a Maratha chief, Govind Pant Bundele, was killed near Meerut. A supply convoy of some 2,000 men was also lost."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Sadashivrao Bhau, 30 years old" },
        "Cousin of the peshwa, young, brave and proud. To his critics, he erred in rejecting guerrilla warfare and in entering with an enormous army, full of civilians. To his defenders, he was pressed by his own chiefs to break out of the siege rather than die of hunger. He was killed in the battle.",
        { h: "Vishwasrao" },
        "Son of the peshwa, he came with the army. His body was found on the field, and his death was one of the turning points of the battle.",
        { h: "Ahmad Shah Durrani" },
        "Founder of the Durrani Empire in 1747 and a veteran of several invasions of India. At Panipat, he was cautious: instead of attacking the Marathas' entrenched camp, he cut their supplies and waited for them to come out.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Maratha side, Ibrahim Khan Gardi commanded the European-trained infantry, the [[gardis|gardis]], and Malharrao Holkar and Mahadji Scindia commanded the cavalry. On the Afghan side, Najib ud-Daulah led the Rohillas, Shah Wali Khan was the vizier, and Shuja-ud-Daula, of Awadh, had his own army. The best account of the battle comes from Kashi Raj, Shuja's secretary, who saw everything from the Afghan side."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The numbers vary widely. The most cited estimate for the Maratha side is 45,000 to 60,000 fighters, among them about 9,000 gardis, plus a crowd of civilians, pilgrims and camp followers: one estimate speaks of about 200,000 non-combatants. For the Afghans and allies, counts range from 60,000 to 100,000.",
        "The Marathas brought about 150 guns, French-made but heavy and almost immobile. The Afghans had some 60 guns, plus 50 to 60 with Shuja-ud-Daula and about 2,000 [[zamburaks|zamburak]], small guns mounted on camels.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "An open, flat plain. The Marathas spent two to three months in an entrenched camp near Panipat, and the Afghan battle line formed south of the camp, where the Sanauli road runs today.",
        { h: "The hunger" },
        "With supplies cut, the Maratha camp's cattle began to die in late December and early January, and the soldiers went hungry. Both sides contested the support of Shuja-ud-Daula, but the negotiations came to nothing. Bhau expected reinforcements from Pune and, after waiting, decided to break the siege, also under pressure from his chiefs. In the early hours of 13 to 14 January, the Marathas left the camp and marched south. The two armies met at about 8 a.m. on the 14th.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, the Marathas formed a line of about 12 km, with the [[artillery|artilharia]] in front, protected by infantry, lancers and archers, and the cavalry behind. Bhau, with the plan of softening the enemy with the guns before releasing the cavalry, stayed in the center, with Vishwasrao. The gardis took the left end, and Holkar and Scindia the right. Behind them came some 30,000 young, inexperienced soldiers, the civilians and a second line of infantry.",
        "On the other side, Najib and Pasand Khan, with 5,000 horsemen, held the Afghan left end. In the center-left, the vizier Shah Wali Khan, with about 19,000 armored cavalry, and Shuja-ud-Daula, with 3,000 soldiers and 50 to 60 guns. In the center-right, some 15,000 Rohillas, under Hafiz Rahmat. On the right end, Barkhurdar Khan and Amir Beg. Ahmad Shah watched from behind the center, with a reserve of some 15,000 men, Qizilbash musketeers and the camels.",
        "Second, the Marathas advanced at about 8 a.m. The artillery opened fire.",
        "Third, Ibrahim Khan Gardi's musketeers, on the left end, attacked the Rohillas and did great damage. Gaikwad and Vinchurkar protected their flanks.",
        "Fourth, in the center, Bhau led a charge against Shah Wali Khan's line and nearly broke it. Shuja-ud-Daula did not enter the fight, and Abdali's musketeers stopped those who wanted to abandon the combat from fleeing.",
        "Fifth, Abdali launched the reserves: cavalry in chain mail and the camels with swivel guns. At about 2 p.m., the fighting was hand to hand.",
        "Sixth, at 4 p.m., the Maratha infantry was exhausted. Vishwasrao was killed, hit in the head. Bhau dismounted and fought on foot, wounded, with three horses killed under him. Gaikwad and Vinchurkar abandoned their positions, and the gardis were left without protection on the flanks.",
        "Seventh, the Maratha line fell apart at sunset. Holkar and Scindia escaped, some groups held out until dark, and many soldiers fled in the night. Bhau's widow, Parvatibai, escaped to Pune under Holkar's protection.",
        "Eighth, the Afghan cavalry pursued the fugitives and overran the camp, where the civilians were. According to the chronicler Kashi Raj, the next day about 40,000 prisoners were massacred.",
        { note: "One account says that Gardi's musketeers killed about 12,000 Rohillas in three hours. It is a high number, it comes from a single account, and it should be read with care." }
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The Afghans won, but the victory was costly. Estimates of the dead differ greatly: one speaks of 60,000 to 70,000 dead counting both sides, another of at least 100,000 Marathas, soldiers and civilians, killed in the battle and after it. One estimate of Afghan and Rohilla casualties speaks of about 20,000.",
        "The worst came afterward. According to Kashi Raj, about 40,000 Maratha prisoners were massacred the next day, and other accounts from the time support the essence of that version. About 22,000 women and children were enslaved, according to one chronicle. About 2,000 captives were freed by Sikhs, led by Jassa Singh Ahluwalia.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The bodies of Bhau and Vishwasrao were found and cremated. The peshwa Balaji Baji Rao, according to the accounts, never recovered from the blow and died on 23 June 1761. Abdali left the region in March 1761, after naming Najib ud-Daulah [[regent|regente]] in Delhi and recognizing Shah Alam II as emperor. The promised tribute was never paid.",
        "The Maratha Empire lost its commander, the peshwa's son and tens of thousands of soldiers, and with them the force of its advance to the north. About ten years later, Mahadji Scindia rebuilt Maratha power in the north and retook Delhi in 1771. The final fall came in the Anglo-Maratha wars, and the power vacuum opened room for British expansion.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The numbers of the dead: the main accounts were written by people from one side or the other, and the totals vary widely. The historian Shejwalkar speaks of no fewer than 100,000 Marathas killed; others give half that or less." },
        { note: "The best testimony of the time is that of Kashi Raj, but he was a secretary at the court of Shuja-ud-Daula, on the winning side. The Jats, who had left the Maratha army before the battle, ended up helping the fleeing Marathas." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  trenton: {
    war: "American War of Independence, New Jersey campaign (1776–1777)",
    hook: "On Christmas night 1776, an army on the edge of collapse crossed an icy river and began to believe in itself again.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In July 1776, the thirteen British colonies of North America declared independence. But declaring was not enough: it was necessary to defeat the greatest military power of the age. By the end of the year, the revolution was close to ending.",
        "George Washington had lost battle after battle in New York: White Plains, Fort Washington, Fort Lee. He retreated across New Jersey to the west bank of the Delaware River, in Pennsylvania, with a few thousand men, many of them without shoes and in tattered uniforms. The enlistments of a good part of them ended at the close of the year, and many were leaving. Washington wrote to a relative that he thought the game was nearly lost.",
        { h: "The road to Trenton" },
        { timeline: [
          ["Jul. 1776", "Declaration of Independence."],
          ["Aug.–Nov. 1776", "Defeats in New York and retreat across New Jersey."],
          ["Dec. 1776", "The American army camps on the west bank of the Delaware."],
          ["25 Dec., at night", "The crossing of the river."],
          ["26 Dec.", "The battle of Trenton."],
          ["2–3 Jan. 1777", "The battles of Assunpink Creek and Princeton."]
        ]},
        "The soldiers' morale was so low that the writings of Thomas Paine, 'The American Crisis', circulated among the campfires to renew their commitment to the cause. On the British side, General William Howe, thinking the campaign practically won, set up a chain of outposts across New Jersey for the winter. At Trenton, a garrison of [[Hessians|hessianos]] was left, German soldiers hired by the British Crown."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "George Washington, 44 years old" },
        "A farmer and officer from Virginia, he commanded the [[Continental Army|exército continental]]. He had not yet won an important battle in the war. At Trenton, his plan was a gamble: with the army coming apart, he needed a quick victory. The password for the operation was 'Victory or Death'.",
        { h: "Johann Rall, 50 years old" },
        "A Hessian colonel, an experienced officer. His superior, Colonel von Donop, ordered him to fortify Trenton. Rall did not obey, partly because he disliked von Donop. He himself wrote that the town was indefensible and asked for reinforcement, with a garrison at Maidenhead, but the request was denied. When he was advised to fortify the town, he answered that if the Americans came, they would get the bayonet. On 22 December, the British general Grant warned him to stay alert. When the first shots sounded on the morning of the 26th, Rall thought it was the attack Grant had spoken of.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the American side: General Nathanael Greene and General John Sullivan commanded the two columns, Henry Knox the artillery, and John Glover, with the Marblehead sailors, the boats. James Monroe, a future president, was wounded in the shoulder. On the Hessian side, Lieutenant Andreas Wiederholdt commanded the outpost on the Pennington road."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Washington planned three simultaneous crossings, and only the main one succeeded. One militia corps, under James Ewing, and another, under John Cadwalader, which was to distract the British at Bordentown, could not cross because of the weather. Washington attacked with about 2,400 men and 18 guns, some 3,000 fewer than the plan had called for.",
        "At Trenton, there were about 1,400 to 1,500 Hessians, in three regiments (Knyphausen, Lossberg and Rall), plus German light infantry, the jägers.",
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "The crossing was made about 14 km (nine miles) north of Trenton. The river, some 300 yards (about 275 meters) wide, was full of ice, and a storm of snow and hail made things worse. The men crossed in [[Durham boats|bote durham]], crewed by Glover's sailors, and ferries carried horses and guns. The landing was supposed to end at midnight, but it only finished around 3 a.m. Nobody died in the crossing.",
        "The army marched at 4 a.m., in snow and freezing rain. Many soldiers had no boots and wrapped their feet in rags. Two men died of cold on the way.",
        { h: "The battle in eight moves" },
        { formation: true },
        "First, at Birmingham, today West Trenton, the columns split. Greene's, with Washington, went east along the Pennington road, to attack Trenton from the north. Sullivan's went southwest, along the River Road, to attack from the south.",
        "Second, at about 8 a.m., the Americans attacked the outpost on the Pennington road. The Hessians fell back in order, firing, and then joined a company of the Lossberg in the north of the town, from where they fought from inside the houses.",
        "Third, Sullivan arrived by the River Road, which had been left unguarded, and blocked the only bridge over Assunpink Creek, the only way out to the south. Fifty jägers who were guarding a house, the Hermitage, fled after a single volley. The American artillery took position at the head of King and Queen streets and began to hit the Hessians.",
        "Fourth, Rall formed his regiment and part of the Lossberg in the lower part of King Street, and the Knyphausen in the lower part of Queen, as a reserve. Rall's attack up the street broke against the American guns and the fire from the houses. On Queen Street, the guns repelled every attempt. A bayonet charge led by John Stark broke the Knyphausen, whose muskets would not fire, probably because of the snow and freezing rain.",
        "Fifth, the Lossberg and Rall's regiment fell back to a field outside the town. Rall attempted a last attack, to the sound of the band, against the American flank on the hills to the north. The Hessians recaptured the guns, but Knox's men took them back. Unable to fire most of their muskets, the formation fell apart, and Rall was mortally wounded.",
        "Sixth, Washington came down from the hills with his troops. Most of the Hessians fell back to an orchard, were surrounded and surrendered. The remains of the Knyphausen marched in the wrong direction, tried the bridge, found it taken and surrendered minutes later.",
        { h: "The end of the day" },
        "By about noon, Washington was already back in Pennsylvania, with the prisoners and the supplies. The Hessians had 22 killed (including Rall), 83 wounded and 896 prisoners, counting the wounded; estimates vary between 800 and 920. On the American side, there were five wounded, among them Monroe, and the two who died of cold. Rall died at night, in his headquarters. Before that, he asked Washington to treat his men well, and Washington agreed.",
        "The Americans captured about 1,000 weapons, the Knyphausen's guns and the Hessians' supplies. The prisoners were taken to Philadelphia, then to Lancaster and, in 1777, to Virginia."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "The cost of Trenton to the Americans was small, but the illness and exhaustion that followed may have killed more men than the battle itself. The victory lifted morale: it strengthened the army, helped win back soldiers and gave confidence to Congress. Howe was baffled by the surprise.",
        "Without the columns that did not cross, Washington could not attack Princeton or New Brunswick at once. But he crossed the Delaware again a week later, offered a bonus of 10 dollars to anyone who stayed in the army for a few more weeks, faced the British at Assunpink Creek and won at Princeton. Then he marched to Morristown, where he spent the winter.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Trenton did not decide the war, but it saved it at a moment when it might have ended. It showed that Washington knew how to turn militias from different colonies into a national force, and that the Continental Army could beat professional European troops.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The drunken Hessians: popular history says the Hessians were sleeping off drink after Christmas. A patriot who watched them, John Greenwood, said nothing was drunk that night, and the historian Edward Lengel also calls the legend false." },
        { note: "The painting: 'Washington Crossing the Delaware', by Emanuel Leutze, dates from 1851 and was painted in Germany, 75 years later. It has the star-spangled flag, which did not yet exist (it was adopted in 1777), and boats that are not Durham boats. It is a symbol, not a portrait of what happened." },
        { note: "The dates and the numbers: the Hessians appear as between 1,400 and 1,500 men, and the prisoners as between 800 and 920, depending on the source. We use the ranges, not a single number." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  saratoga: {
    war: "American War of Independence, Hudson campaign (1777)",
    hook: "An entire British army surrendered, and a revolt of colonies became a world war.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 1777, the British government had a plan to end the revolt: to separate New England from the other colonies by controlling the Hudson River. Three forces would advance. General John Burgoyne would come down from Canada by Lake Champlain and the Hudson to Albany. Colonel Barry St. Leger would come from the west, by the Mohawk River valley. And General William Howe would go up the Hudson from New York.",
        "In practice, it went wrong. Howe decided to go to Philadelphia, by sea, instead of going up the river. St. Leger gave up after a failed siege of Fort Stanwix. And Burgoyne advanced slowly, with a column full of guns and baggage, through dense forest, with supply problems.",
        { h: "The road to Saratoga" },
        { timeline: [
          ["6 Jul. 1777", "Burgoyne retakes Fort Ticonderoga."],
          ["16 Aug.", "The British lose almost 1,000 men at Bennington."],
          ["19 Aug.", "Horatio Gates takes over American command."],
          ["19 Sep.", "First battle: Freeman's Farm."],
          ["6 Oct.", "Clinton takes Forts Clinton and Montgomery, on the Hudson."],
          ["7 Oct.", "Second battle: Bemis Heights."],
          ["17 Oct.", "Burgoyne surrenders."]
        ]},
        "Burgoyne's situation worsened after Bennington, where he lost almost 1,000 men, on 16 August. His Indigenous allies, for the most part, left. On the American side, General Philip Schuyler had retreated after the loss of Ticonderoga, and on 19 August he was replaced by Horatio Gates. The region was roused by the murder of Jane McCrea, a young colonist, attributed to Burgoyne's Indigenous allies, and more militiamen came forward.",
        "Washington sent north Benedict Arnold, Benjamin Lincoln and Daniel Morgan's [[riflemen|fuzileiros]]. Tadeusz Kościuszko, a Polish engineer, fortified Bemis Heights, a hill by the river that barred the road to Albany."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "The main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "John Burgoyne, 55 years old" },
        "An elegant British general, a playwright, known as 'Gentleman Johnny'. At Saratoga, he was stuck in a forest, without the other two forces of the plan, which never arrived. After the surrender, he returned to England and never again held a field command.",
        { h: "Horatio Gates" },
        "A former British officer, he commanded the American army of the north and took most of the credit for the victory. He lost badly at Camden, in 1780, and never again commanded troops in the field.",
        { h: "Benedict Arnold" },
        "One of the most aggressive fighters on the American side. He quarreled with Gates, who left Arnold out of the Freeman's Farm report and later took his field command away. On 7 October, he rode out to the fight, and there is debate over whether it was with or without Gates's authorization. He was wounded, and years later, in 1780, he would betray the cause at West Point.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the British side, Brigadier Simon Fraser commanded the army's elite troops, General Friedrich Riedesel the German soldiers, General James Inglis Hamilton the center of the column at Freeman's Farm, and General William Phillips the artillery. On the American side, Daniel Morgan and Henry Dearborn led the riflemen and the light infantry, and Generals Enoch Poor and Ebenezer Learned commanded brigades."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Counts differ. On 19 September, there were about 9,000 Americans against some 7,200 British. On 7 October, Burgoyne had some 5,000 men fit to fight (the highest count says 6,600), while the Americans had more than 8,000 in the field, and more than 12,000 in all. They were British soldiers, Germans from Brunswick and Hesse-Hanau, Canadians and Indigenous allies, against the Continental Army and the militia.",
        { h: "19 September: Freeman's Farm" },
        "Burgoyne crossed the Hudson and advanced in three columns. The left one, Riedesel's, went along the river road, protecting the guns and supplies. The center one, Hamilton's, was to attack the heights. The right one, Fraser's, was to go around the American left through the wooded hills.",
        "Arnold, who commanded the American left wing, wanted to meet the maneuver in the forest, where the Americans fought better. Gates only authorized a [[reconnaissance in force|reconhecimento armado]], with Morgan's riflemen and Dearborn's light infantry. Near a clearing, Freeman's farm, they met the lead company of Hamilton's column. The marksmen hit most of the officers, and Morgan's men advanced. Then Fraser's vanguard struck Morgan's left and drove them back into the woods.",
        "At about 1 p.m., there was a pause. Then the fighting continued in waves. Morgan's riflemen cut down British gunners several times, and the Americans took the guns several times, only to lose them again. The British center nearly split before Phillips brought the 20th regiment forward. At about 3 p.m., Riedesel arrived with part of his column and fired on the American right. Gates sent reinforcements to Arnold. Night ended the fight, the Americans went back to their lines and the British kept the field.",
        "Burgoyne held the ground, but paid dearly: almost 600 casualties, most of them in the center, where the 62nd regiment was reduced to a single company. American losses were almost 300 killed and seriously wounded. Technically, a British victory; in practice, the army came out much weaker.",
        { h: "The interval" },
        "Burgoyne waited for help from Henry Clinton, who was in New York. A letter from Clinton said he would attack Fort Montgomery about ten days later. On 3 October, Clinton set out, and on 6 October he took Forts Clinton and Montgomery, on the Hudson, but it was too late to help Burgoyne. In the meantime, Burgoyne put his army on short rations on 3 October. Riedesel and Fraser advised retreat, but Burgoyne decided to attack.",
        { h: "7 October: Bemis Heights" },
        "At about 10 or 11 a.m., Burgoyne set out with Riedesel and Fraser, with a force of light troops and the 24th regiment on the right, grenadiers on the left and Germans in the center, and with eight British guns and two German ones. They advanced some 1,200 meters to a wheat field, Barber's, and stopped to observe the American line.",
        "Gates sent Morgan along the far left, Poor along the left, the New York regiments along the right and Learned's brigade through the center. At about 2 p.m., Major Acland led a bayonet charge of the grenadiers, but Poor let them come close, and the column fell apart. Acland was wounded in both legs and captured, along with Major Williams, and the Americans took the guns. On the British left, Morgan pushed back the Canadians and the Indigenous allies and engaged Fraser's regulars. Fraser was mortally wounded, and Burgoyne escaped narrowly, with his horse, hat and waistcoat hit. The first phase lasted about an hour and cost Burgoyne almost 400 men and six of his ten guns.",
        "Then Arnold, who no longer had a field command, rushed into the fight. The British right rested on two [[redoubts|reduto]]: Balcarres's, which resisted fiercely, and Heinrich von Breymann's, with some 300 men. Arnold led Poor's men against Balcarres's redoubt and Learned's through the gap between the two redoubts. Morgan's went around and reached Breymann's rear. Breymann's redoubt fell, and Breymann died defending it. Arnold had his horse killed and his leg broken, the same leg already wounded at Quebec, in 1775.",
        { h: "The siege and the surrender" },
        "On the night of 7 October, Burgoyne lit fires at the front and withdrew in the dark, between 16 and 24 km north, toward Saratoga, today Schuylerville. He was surrounded by a much larger army. On 13 October, he called a council of war. Riedesel proposed that the soldiers be released on parole, without weapons, and march back to Canada, and Burgoyne asked for transport from Boston to Europe. After days of negotiation, they signed the [[convention|convenção]]. On 17 October, Burgoyne's army surrendered."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Between the two fights, Burgoyne lost more than 1,000 men. Those who surrendered numbered about 5,800, and another count gives 6,222. American losses were far smaller: about 330 killed and wounded, in the most cited counts.",
        "Burgoyne insisted on calling the agreement a convention, and under its terms his soldiers were to return to Europe, under a promise not to fight again in America. Congress later refused to honor the agreement, and the so-called Convention Army remained prisoner until the end of the war. Burgoyne returned to England. Gates kept the credit, Arnold had his seniority restored, but spent months recovering.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "France was already helping the Americans in secret, with arms and ammunition: the Vallière guns used at Saratoga were French. News of the surrender reached Paris in December 1777 and helped convince the French government. In February 1778, France signed an alliance with the United States and entered the war. Great Britain had to divert troops and ships to the West Indies and to Europe, and Spain entered the war in 1779. A revolt of colonies became a world conflict.",
        "Congress declared 18 December 1777 a day of thanksgiving for the victory.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Who shot Fraser? Tradition says it was Timothy Murphy, one of Morgan's marksmen. Some historians think this story may be a 19th-century invention." },
        { note: "Arnold's role: the sources disagree about what he did at Freeman's Farm. Luzader thinks he stayed at headquarters; Martin argues that he directed troops and perhaps led charges. On 7 October, there is a letter suggesting that Gates authorized him, but others say he went without authorization." },
        { note: "The Boot Monument, at Saratoga, honors Arnold's leg, wounded in the battle, without naming him. Years later, in 1780, he would betray the American cause at West Point." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

